 these kinds of questions have concrete  answers and thinking about those answers  is actual problem solving  where problem solving goes wrong is when  you begin thinking about what if and why  these are not answerable questions on  our own because we can't know the future  or know why others think or do what they  do  speculating answers to these is not  solving a problem or are making progress  toward a goal  it's problem solving gone wrong so if  you spiral into questions like what if  my performance isn't meeting  expectations  what if my colleagues are all making  higher salaries than i am  why was jennifer given the bigger  project last month  in control theory  overthinking starts to take place  because we're aware that we're not  meeting the goal  or believe that not enough progress has  been made toward a goal we know there's  a problem but our thinking can't solve  it so we end up overthinking  unproductively  psychologists tested this idea directly  in 2013  they tested two groups on a number of  tasks after asking them to think about  an issue that had concerned them  the control group was asked to think  about a concern that had previously  troubled them but had been resolved  the experimental group was asked to  think about a concern that was an  unresolved issue  one that had repeatedly come into their  mind and caused them to feel negative or  stressed during the previous week  as predicted cueing unresolved goals  resulted in significantly greater  recurrent unwanted intrusive thoughts  than cueing resolved goals during  subsequent tasks  and the more any individual reported  overthinking in general in their lives  the more likely they were to be  influenced by that queuing  overthinking is not just triggered by  unresolved issues it becomes a habit  over time too  so let's think about it  within this model thinking about an  issue is not a problem  attempting to problem solve  is not a problem  the problem has to do with the goal  goals that are unresolved or hard to  make progress toward are likely to lead  to overthinking  we need to recognize that goals that are  impossible or are nearly impossible to  meet are going to be a problem  hence the question we need to ask  ourselves  is reaching my goal under my control  according to control theory if your goal  is something that is not under your  control or not something that you have  information about or could get clear  information on how to take steps towards  this can trigger overthinking  impossible goals  are more common than you might think  do any of these sound familiar  i want you to love my work  problem i don't have control over your  emotions  i want to be happy all the time  problem i can't control my mood all the  time  i don't want to think about my ex  problem i can't control the thoughts  that habitually pop into my  head we want things  we have these desires quickly  automatically out of habit and often out  of awareness  they cue thinking  and that can quickly cascade into  overthinking  the exercise that you worked with helps  with this reality  sense store labeling puts thinking on  the same level as anything else hearing  seeing smelling digesting  it makes the content of the thought goal  or otherwise not so important  it's just a thought  and turning towards these automatic  habitual thoughts can help us create a  new habit of interrupting the spiral  you may not have ever considered  what you want or what you don't want as  a goal that you're working toward  nor considered the reality that most of  what you want  is beyond your control  that's because the habit is ingrained  it started early  from the beginning of our lives we have  constantly tried to assess whether and  to what extent it's safe for us to try  and pursue goals  how we go about this became a habit  we quickly learn contingencies that we  detect in the world and if we had to  speak them they end up sounding a lot  like if then statements  if i cry  then my mother will come and soothe me  goal we want to be soothed the action we  take  crying  if i smile at my caregiver then they  will smile back at me  goal  we want affection  action  we smile at the person that we want  affection from  this pattern of understanding is  developed early during a childhood  period that's prone to  over-generalization  lack of experience  and the illusion that we can control how  another person responds to us  but in reality  we cannot control others  let's consider some if then statements  that reflect our actions as adults  if i make this person laugh  then they'll want to go out with me  goal we want someone to go out with us  problem  this is beyond our control  if i show that i have a plan for the  company's future in the staff meeting  then i will not get laid off  goal  i want to keep my job  problem  hiring and firing decisions are beyond  my control  we even have goals that attempt to  change the past  if i had worked less and spent more time  going on ski trips with my ex  we would still be together  problem  changing the past is not under our  control  again  we want something we begin thinking in  order to problem solve  but then one thought spirals into many  because it's cueing the fact that we've  not met our goal or have not made enough  progress towards it  this thinking is not problem-solving  it's overthinking  unfortunately  just understanding this cause of  overthinking doesn't make it go away  understanding in and of itself isn't  enough  we need to use what we understand to  figure out what action to take  we can re-pattern the habits of how we  think  for example using mindfulness exercises  like labeling sense doors that we began  with  we will also learn other techniques  including physical activities and  psychological tools that can help  but in the moment of overthinking at its  most basic  we can also just pause  pay attention to our thoughts  and ask ourselves a question  is there a problem that exists now  in the external world around you  if there is  can you do something to change it  now  if the answer is yes and yes  your thinking will be concrete problem  solving  go  solve the problem  but if the answer to even one of the  questions is no  then you're going to be likely to  overthink  we need to work with changing the habits  of how we think  that is what this course will help you  to do
 

